<Article
  title="One room, several lives"
  description="Return to Aura. One beautiful room at a time."
  kicker="The story"
  date="2026-09-30"
>
  <p>Aura starts as a finished room you can add to land you already have. It sits on a steel frame. The glass is smooth: clear, mirror, or tint. That room is the product. Everything else on this site is a way of using it.</p>
  <RevealHeading as="h2">What the room can be</RevealHeading>
  <p>As a room, it is an extension or a small stay. As glass, it lifts onto helical piles, with a floor lit from beneath and a deck. As a resort, a few of those rooms are planned for a site in Canada or Costa Rica, built custom. Costa Rica does not have its photograph yet. The snow is the reference until it does.</p>
  <p>As a farm, one long side becomes a vertical aeroponic wall. As Thirst Contact, an atmospheric generator would sit on the roof and a short bottling line would sit inside. That water unit comes later, and it still needs further research. The work changes. The room stays a room.</p>
  <RevealHeading as="h2">What you can do now</RevealHeading>
  <p>You can ask Aura to build a room, or a farm unit, and place it. You can buy one and use it yourself: live in it, or grow in it. Bottling waits on Thirst Contact. The source for this site is on <a href="https://github.com/kr8tiv-ai/aura-homes">GitHub</a>.</p>
  <RevealHeading as="h2">What we hope</RevealHeading>
  <p>The longer hope is a network in which food and water from these rooms are free to the person who takes them, and an advertiser’s name is on the harvest or the jug. That network is described in <a href="/journal/free-food-and-water">its own note</a>. It is not operating today. Saying so plainly is part of the offer.</p>
  <p>If you want the room first, start at <a href="/#shell">the room</a>. If you want the farm or the water, those pages say what is added, and what is still a hope.</p>
</Article>
